[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one parser bug, one fix queued. Read the reports
instead of their subjects and the ticket holds three problems. Each needs its own fix, so each now
has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first row below. The other two keep coming after it ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Our tenants all write the day first,
so 7 April goes out as 4 July. Every channel, every tenant. This is what #4471 says it is, and the
queued fix is for this.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells day-first people to type month first.** Its date field prints
`MM/DD/YYYY`, people type what it says, and the form then reads the date the tenant's way. Only
tenants on booking-v3, and only reports that came in through the form. Fixing the parser does not
touch this: the form needs a hint in the tenant's own format. The 4471 page already shows this
row as "not this ticket, no ticket of its own".

**`shift`: a booking lands one day early or late.** "Invite says Tuesday 12 May, calendar says
Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday." "The invite arrived
before I made the booking." No flipped date does that; a time zone does. These are the reports the
desk already listed as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`). The 4471 page counts them under
`parser` and footnotes them. They stay counted there, the desk's way, and get their own row and
their own ticket as well.

## How each report is placed

1. Count each report once: the export repeats some rows (`uniqueById`).
2. Every report goes where its tenant puts it (`causes.ts`), as in the desk's own view: through the
   form on booking-v3 is `hint`, anything else is `parser`.
3. Reports the desk read as a one-day move are counted under `shift` too.

The rule lives in `#lib/kb/problems.ts`. The table above is rebuilt from the export on every build,
so the counts follow the desk as it keeps merging.

## What intake got wrong

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser. The cause is the tenant's: their locale and
their form. The "intake tagged" column shows how often those disagree: `hint` reports tagged
`parser` and the other way round. Don't triage on the tag.

## Why it took until now

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subjects match.
"Wrong day on the invite" is the subject of all three problems, so all three went into one
ticket and the count looked like one loud bug. Merging on subject is fast. It is not the same as
merging on cause: [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) has an explorer that shows the difference.

